ot a "},{"code":"Graph","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":","},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"and no shared parent with one: a hyperedge is not an edge. The "},{"code":"Hypergraphs","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" module’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"incidenceGraphView","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" reads a hypergraph as its bipartite incidence graph (the Levi graph), which is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"how the graph algorithms reach it."}]},"doc://Hypergraphs/documentation/Hypergraphs/IncidenceGraphView":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Hypergraphs\/documentation\/Hypergraphs\/IncidenceGraphView","role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"An undirected hypergraph’s incidence graph, read lazily from its storage: a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Graph"},{"type":"text","text":" on"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0 ..< n + m"},{"type":"text","text":" whose vertex "},{"code":"i < n","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is the hypergraph’s vertex index ","type":"text"},{"code":"i","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and whose vertex ","type":"text"},{"code":"n + p","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"is hyperedge ","type":"text"},{"code":"p","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", with an edge ","type":"text"},{"code":"{i, n + p}","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" for each incidence. It is bipartite and simple","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"(vertices on the left, hyperedges on the right), an empty hyperedge is an isolated vertex, and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"it loses nothing.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"IncidenceGraphView","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/hypergraphs\/incidencegraphview","title":"IncidenceGraphView","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"IncidenceGraphView","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://Hypergraphs/do